# Introduction to Solid State Physics (Degen, HS26): Weekly Problem Sets 4-6, condensed from the official sheets and solutions.
# Style rule for these files: no ASCII apostrophes inside r'...' strings (use ’ in text, ^\prime in math).
from ss_sheet_lib import S, Q, F

EX = lambda n, q: {'file': f'Exercises/Weekly Problem Set {n}.pdf', 'where': f'Weekly Problem Set {n}, Exercise {q}; official solution: Weekly Solution Set {n}.pdf'}

S(4, 'Phonons and elasticity, the Einstein model', '2026-10-16', [
 Q(1, 'A model for lattice vibrations: phonons and elasticity', 'phonon-halls', ['ss-pho-L1', 'ss-pho-D1', 'ss-pho-c1', 'ss-pho-b1'],
   r'Crystal planes of atoms of mass $M$, displacement $u_n(t)$ of plane $n$, force per atom $F_n=\sum_sC_s(u_{n+s}-u_n)+C_s(u_{n-s}-u_n)$ ($C_s$: force constant to the $s$-th neighbour plane). (a) Solve with $u_n=Ae^{i(nka-\omega t)}$ and find $\omega(k)$. (b) Nearest neighbours only: sketch $\omega(k)$; meaning of the slope at $k\to0$; the wave and the slope at $k\to\pm\pi/a$; allowed $k$ for $N$ planes ($u_0=u_N$); are there vibrations with $k>\pi/a$; how many modes; sketch for $N=3,10,30$. (c) Given a measured $\omega(k)$, how do you get the $C_s$? (d) Show that for long wavelengths the equation of motion becomes $\frac1{c^2}\partial_t^2u=\partial_x^2u$; relate $c$ and the elastic modulus to the $C_s$. (e) GaAs: lattice constant $d=5.65$ Å, $c=4.73\times10^5$ cm/s for LA phonons along (100). Estimate $C_1$.',
   [(r'(a) Dispersion.', r'$M\ddot u_n=\sum_sC_s(u_{n+s}+u_{n-s}-2u_n)$. Ansatz: $-M\omega^2=\sum_sC_s(e^{iska}+e^{-iska}-2)$, so $\omega^2=\sum_s\frac{2C_s}{M}[1-\cos(ska)]=\sum_s\frac{4C_s}{M}\sin^2\frac{ska}{2}$.'),
    (r'(b) Nearest neighbours.', r'$\omega=\sqrt{\frac{4C}{M}}\left|\sin\frac{ka}2\right|$. $k\to0$: $\omega=\sqrt{C/M}\,ka$, slope = speed of sound $c=\sqrt{Ca^2/M}$. $k\to\pm\pi/a$: $u_n=A(-1)^ne^{-i\omega t}$, neighbours in antiphase, a standing wave, $d\omega/dk=0$ (group velocity zero). Periodic boundary: $e^{iNka}=1$, $k_p=\frac{2\pi}{Na}p$. $k^\prime=k+2\pi/a$ gives the same displacements, $e^{ink^\prime a}=e^{inka}$: everything folds back into the first Brillouin zone. $-\frac N2<p\le\frac N2$: exactly $N$ modes.<br>' + F('s4q1-discrete-dispersion', 'Dispersion of the monatomic chain sampled at the allowed k values for N = 3, 10 and 30: the points fill the sine curve more and more densely', 300)),
    (r'(c) Force constants from the dispersion.', r'$\omega^2(k)$ is even and periodic with $2\pi/a$: a Fourier cosine series. Multiply by $\cos(nka)$ and integrate over the zone: $\int_{-\pi/a}^{\pi/a}dk\,\omega^2(k)\cos(nka)=-\frac{2\pi C_n}{Ma}$, hence $C_n=-\frac{Ma}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi/a}^{\pi/a}dk\,\omega^2(k)\cos(nka)$.'),
    (r'(d) Continuum limit.', r'For $ka\ll1/s$: $\cos(ska)\approx1-\frac12(ska)^2$, $\omega^2=k^2\frac{a^2}{M}\sum_sC_ss^2$. A plane wave $u=Ae^{i(kx-\omega t)}$ has $\partial_t^2u=-\omega^2u$, $\partial_x^2u=-k^2u$, so $\frac1{c^2}\partial_t^2u=\partial_x^2u$ with $c^2=\frac{a^2}{M}\sum_sC_ss^2$. (Equivalently: Taylor-expand $u_{n\pm s}=u(x_n\pm sa)$ to second order in the equation of motion.) Continuum mechanics: $c=\sqrt{E/\rho}$, so $E=\rho c^2=\frac{\rho a^2}{M}\sum_sC_ss^2$.'),
    (r'(e) GaAs.', r'$C_1=\frac{Mc^2}{a^2}$. Zincblende: the spacing of equivalent (100) planes is half the lattice constant, $a=d/2=2.825$ Å, and $M=m_{\rm Ga}+m_{\rm As}=144.6\,u=2.40\times10^{-25}$ kg. $C_1=\frac{2.40\times10^{-25}\cdot(4730)^2}{(2.825\times10^{-10})^2}\approx67\ \mathrm{N/m}$.')],
   [r'$\omega^2=\sum_s\frac{4C_s}M\sin^2(ska/2)$', r'slope at $k\to0$ is the sound velocity; standing wave at the zone boundary', r'$k_p=2\pi p/Na$, $N$ modes, folding into the 1st BZ', r'$C_n$ as Fourier coefficients of $\omega^2(k)$', r'$c^2=\frac{a^2}M\sum_sC_ss^2$, $E=\rho c^2$', r'GaAs: $a=d/2$, $M=m_{\rm Ga}+m_{\rm As}$, $C_1\approx67$ N/m'], xp=60, source=EX(4, 1)),
 Q(2, 'Einstein model for the heat capacity', 'phonon-halls', ['ss-pho-L2', 'ss-pho-c3', 'ss-pho-Q1'],
   r'Einstein model: a single frequency $\omega(k)=\omega_E$ (like one optical branch). (a) Thermal energy of a crystal with $N$ primitive cells (use the Bose–Einstein occupation). (b) Specific heat. (c) Limits $T\gg\Theta_E$ and $T\ll\Theta_E$, $\Theta_E=\hbar\omega_E/k$; interpret.',
   [(r'(a) Energy.', r'$U=\sum_k\hbar\omega(k)\left[n(k)+\frac12\right]=\int d\omega\,Z(\omega)\,\hbar\omega\left(\frac12+\frac{1}{e^{\hbar\omega/kT}-1}\right)$ with $Z(\omega)=3N\delta(\omega-\omega_E)$: $U=3N\hbar\omega_E\left(\frac12+\frac1{e^{\hbar\omega_E/kT}-1}\right)$.'),
    (r'(b) Specific heat.', r'$c(T)=\frac1V\frac{dU}{dT}=\frac{3Nk}{V}\left(\frac{\hbar\omega_E}{kT}\right)^2\frac{e^{\hbar\omega_E/kT}}{\left(e^{\hbar\omega_E/kT}-1\right)^2}$.'),
    (r'(c) Limits.', r'$T\gg\Theta_E$: with $x=\hbar\omega_E/kT\to0$, $x^2e^x/(e^x-1)^2\to1$: $c\to\frac{3Nk}{V}$, Dulong–Petit (every oscillator classical). $T\ll\Theta_E$: $c\approx\frac{3Nk}{V}\left(\frac{\Theta_E}{T}\right)^2e^{-\Theta_E/T}$: exponentially frozen out, because the first excited level is a finite gap $\hbar\omega_E$ away. Real crystals show $T^3$ instead (acoustic phonons have no gap: Debye). Typical $\Theta_E$: several hundred K; GaAs optical phonons $\approx35$ meV, $\Theta_E\approx420$ K, a small contribution even at room temperature.')],
   [r'$U=3N\hbar\omega_E(\frac12+n_B)$', r'$c=\frac{3Nk}{V}x^2e^x/(e^x-1)^2$, $x=\Theta_E/T$', r'high $T$: Dulong–Petit $3Nk/V$', r'low $T$: $\propto e^{-\Theta_E/T}$ because of the gap'], source=EX(4, 2)),
 Q(3, 'Total heat capacity of a diatomic material', 'phonon-halls', ['ss-pho-L2', 'ss-pho-c3', 'ss-pho-L1'],
   r'A lithium crystal with $N$ atoms: 7.5% $^6$Li and 92.5% $^7$Li. Extend the Einstein model to two masses. (a) Same spring constant $k$ for all atoms: the two eigenfrequencies? (b) Energy stored in the vibrations of the $^6$Li atoms, of the $^7$Li atoms, and in total at temperature $T$. (c) Heat capacity as a function of $T$. Below what temperature are nearly all atoms in the ground state?',
   [(r'(a) Frequencies.', r'$\omega_{1,2}=\sqrt{k/m_{1,2}}$ with $m_1=6u$, $m_2=7u$: $\omega_1/\omega_2=\sqrt{7/6}=1.08$.'),
    (r'(b) Energies.', r'One atom is a 3D oscillator: $E_{1,2}=3\left(n(\omega_{1,2})+\frac12\right)\hbar\omega_{1,2}$, $n(\omega)=\frac1{e^{\hbar\omega/k_BT}-1}$. Total: $E=N_1E_1+N_2E_2=\frac{3N\hbar}{100}\left[7.5\left(n(\omega_1)+\frac12\right)\omega_1+92.5\left(n(\omega_2)+\frac12\right)\omega_2\right]$.'),
    (r'(c) Heat capacity.', r'$C=\frac{dE}{dT}=3Nk_B\left[0.075\,f\!\left(\frac{\hbar\omega_1}{k_BT}\right)+0.925\,f\!\left(\frac{\hbar\omega_2}{k_BT}\right)\right]$ with the Einstein function $f(x)=\frac{x^2e^x}{(e^x-1)^2}$ (per volume: divide by $V$). An oscillator freezes into its ground state below its Einstein temperature. On cooling, the higher frequency ($^6$Li, $\omega_1$) freezes first; all atoms are mostly in the ground state below the <b>lower</b> one, $T\lesssim\hbar\omega_2/k_B$ ($^7$Li).')],
   [r'$\omega_{1,2}=\sqrt{k/m_{1,2}}$', r'$E=N_1E_1+N_2E_2$ with 3 oscillators per atom', r'sum of two Einstein functions weighted 7.5% and 92.5%', r'ground state below the lower Einstein temperature $\hbar\omega_2/k_B$'], source=EX(4, 3)),
])

S(5, 'Debye model in 1D, 2D, 3D and the Debye–Waller factor', '2026-10-23', [
 Q(1, 'Density of states for the linear dispersion of the Debye model', 'phonon-halls', ['ss-pho-L2', 'ss-pho-c2', 'ss-fer-D1'],
   r'(a) Write down the dispersion of the Debye model. (b) Periodic boundary conditions: distance between neighbouring $k$-points and the density of $k$-points in 1, 2, 3 dimensions (sizes $L$, $L^2$, $L^3$). (c) Number of states with $0<\omega<\omega_0$ as an integral over $k$-space in 1D, 2D, 3D, with $p_d$ polarisations. (d) Turn them into integrals over $\omega$ and read off the density of states.',
   [(r'(a) Dispersion.', r'$\omega=v_s|\mathbf k|$: the linear, low-$k$ part of the acoustic branches, used for all $k$.'),
    (r'(b) $k$-points.', r'$e^{ikL}=1$: spacing $2\pi/L$. Densities $\frac{L}{2\pi}$, $\frac{L^2}{(2\pi)^2}$, $\frac{L^3}{(2\pi)^3}$.'),
    (r'(c) Counting.', r'With $k_0=\omega_0/v_s$: $N_{1D}=p_1\frac{L}{2\pi}\int_0^{k_0}2\,dk$ (the 2: $+k$ and $-k$), $N_{2D}=p_2\left(\frac{L}{2\pi}\right)^2\int_0^{k_0}2\pi k\,dk$, $N_{3D}=p_3\left(\frac{L}{2\pi}\right)^3\int_0^{k_0}4\pi k^2\,dk$.'),
    (r'(d) Density of states.', r'$k=\omega/v_s$, $dk=d\omega/v_s$: $g_{1D}(\omega)=\frac{p_1L}{\pi v_s}$ (constant), $g_{2D}(\omega)=\frac{p_2L^2}{2\pi v_s^2}\,\omega$, $g_{3D}(\omega)=\frac{p_3L^3}{2\pi^2v_s^3}\,\omega^2$; integration from 0 to $\omega_0$. In $d$ dimensions $g\propto\omega^{d-1}$.')],
   [r'$\omega=v_s|\mathbf k|$', r'$k$-spacing $2\pi/L$, density $(L/2\pi)^d$', r'shell volumes $2dk$, $2\pi k\,dk$, $4\pi k^2dk$', r'$g\propto\omega^{d-1}$: constant, $\propto\omega$, $\propto\omega^2$'], source=EX(5, 1)),
 Q(2, 'Debye model in 2D', 'phonon-halls', ['ss-pho-L2', 'ss-pho-c2', 'ss-pho-c3', 'ss-deg-D1'],
   r'A solid so thin that phonons propagate only in two dimensions (isotropic Debye model). (a) Integral expression for the vibrational energy as a function of $T$. (b) Heat capacity in the high-$T$ limit. (c) At low $T$ show $C_V=KT^n$; find $n$ and $K$ (as an integral).',
   [(r'(a) Energy.', r'$E=\frac{p_2L^2}{(2\pi)^2}\int_0^{k_D}\left(n_B+\frac12\right)\hbar vk\,2\pi k\,dk=\frac{p_2L^2}{2\pi}\int_0^{k_D}\frac{\hbar vk^2}{e^{\beta\hbar vk}-1}dk+E_0$. With $x=\beta\hbar vk$: $E=\frac{p_2L^2}{2\pi v^2\hbar^2\beta^3}\int_0^{x_D}\frac{x^2}{e^x-1}dx+E_0$, $x_D=\beta\hbar\omega_D$.'),
    (r'(b) High $T$.', r'$\beta\to0$: $n_B\approx\frac{k_BT}{\hbar\omega}$, so $E\approx\int_0^{\omega_D}k_BT\,g(\omega)\,d\omega=N_{\rm modes}k_BT$ and $C_V=N_{\rm modes}k_B=p_2N_{\rm atoms}k_B$: $2k_B$ per atom for $p_2=2$ (in 3D: $3k_B$, Dulong–Petit). The dimension enters only through the number of modes.'),
    (r'(c) Low $T$.', r'High-energy modes are frozen, so $x_D\to\infty$: $E=\frac{p_2L^2k_B^3T^3}{2\pi v^2\hbar^2}\int_0^\infty\frac{x^2}{e^x-1}dx+E_0$, hence $C_V=KT^2$, $n=2$, $K=\frac{3p_2L^2k_B^3}{2\pi v^2\hbar^2}\int_0^\infty\frac{x^2}{e^x-1}dx$ ($=\frac{3L^2k_B^3}{\pi v^2\hbar^2}\cdot2\zeta(3)$ for $p_2=2$; $\int_0^\infty\frac{x^2}{e^x-1}dx=2\zeta(3)\approx2.404$). In $d$ dimensions $C_V\propto T^d$.')],
   [r'$E=\frac{p_2L^2}{2\pi v^2\hbar^2\beta^3}\int_0^{x_D}\frac{x^2}{e^x-1}dx$', r'high $T$: $C_V=N_{\rm modes}k_B$', r'low $T$: upper limit $\to\infty$', r'$C_V\propto T^2$ in 2D'], source=EX(5, 2)),
 Q(3, 'Anisotropic sound velocities', 'phonon-halls', ['ss-pho-L2', 'ss-pho-c2', 'ss-deg-c5'],
   r'A 3D solid with $v_x\ne v_y\ne v_z$: $\omega=\sqrt{v_x^2k_x^2+v_y^2k_y^2+v_z^2k_z^2}$. (a) Thermal energy as an integral over $k$-space. (b) Substitute to restore spherical symmetry; write the integral in spherical coordinates. (c) Heat capacity; extract its $T$ dependence at low $T$. Does the scaling differ from the isotropic case?',
   [(r'(a) Energy.', r'$E=p_3\left(\frac L{2\pi}\right)^3\int\frac{\hbar\omega(\mathbf k)}{e^{\beta\hbar\omega(\mathbf k)}-1}dk_xdk_ydk_z+E_0$ (the polarisation does not change the dispersion here).'),
    (r'(b) Substitution.', r'$\kappa_i=v_ik_i$: $\omega=|\boldsymbol\kappa|=\kappa$ and $dk_xdk_ydk_z=\frac{d^3\kappa}{v_xv_yv_z}$. $E=p_3\left(\frac L{2\pi}\right)^3\frac1{v_xv_yv_z}\int_0^{\kappa_D}\frac{\hbar\kappa}{e^{\beta\hbar\kappa}-1}4\pi\kappa^2d\kappa+E_0=\frac{p_3L^3}{2\pi^2\hbar^3\beta^4}\frac{1}{v_xv_yv_z}\int_0^{\beta\hbar\kappa_D}\frac{x^3}{e^x-1}dx+E_0$ with $x=\beta\hbar\kappa$.'),
    (r'(c) Heat capacity.', r'Low $T$: $\beta\hbar\kappa_D\to\infty$, $\int_0^\infty\frac{x^3}{e^x-1}dx=\frac{\pi^4}{15}$. $C=\frac{dE}{dT}=\frac{2p_3k_B^4L^3T^3}{\pi^2\hbar^3}\frac1{v_xv_yv_z}\int_0^\infty\frac{x^3}{e^x-1}dx$. Still $T^3$: the only change is $1/v^3\to1/(v_xv_yv_z)$.')],
   [r'$E$ as a $k$-space integral with $n_B$', r'$\kappa_i=v_ik_i$, Jacobian $1/(v_xv_yv_z)$', r'$x=\beta\hbar\kappa$, upper limit $\to\infty$', r'$C\propto T^3/(v_xv_yv_z)$: same $T^3$ law'], source=EX(5, 3)),
 Q(4, 'Influence of temperature on diffraction: the Debye–Waller factor', 'phonon-halls', ['ss-lat-L2', 'ss-pho-L1', 'ss-pho-c4', 'ss-lat-Q1'],
   r'Thermal motion changes the diffraction pattern. Start from $S(\vec G)=\sum_i\gamma_i\,\overline{e^{i\vec G\cdot(\vec r_i+\vec u)}}=\left(\sum_i\gamma_ie^{i\vec G\cdot\vec r_i}\right)\overline{e^{i\vec G\cdot\vec u}}$ ($\vec u(t)$: instantaneous displacement, bar: time average). (a) Derive $I=D(T)I_0$ with $D(T)=e^{-|\vec G|^2\overline{u^2}/3}$. Assume $\vec G\cdot\vec u\ll1$, expand to three terms, atoms oscillate independently. (b) Evaluate $D$ for the $\vec G=\frac{2\pi}{a}(1,1,0)$ reflection of niobium (BCC) at the melting point, using Lindemann: the crystal melts when the RMS amplitude is about 20% of the nearest-neighbour distance.',
   [(r'(a) Derivation.', r'$\overline{e^{i\vec G\cdot\vec u}}\approx1+i\,\overline{\vec G\cdot\vec u}-\frac12\overline{(\vec G\cdot\vec u)^2}$. Random, independent motion: $\overline{\vec G\cdot\vec u}=0$ and $\overline{(\vec G\cdot\vec u)^2}=|\vec G|^2\,\overline{u^2}\,\overline{\cos^2\Theta}$ with $\overline{\cos^2\Theta}=\frac1{4\pi}\int_0^{\pi}d\Theta\int_0^{2\pi}d\Phi\cos^2\Theta\sin\Theta=\frac13$. So $\overline{e^{i\vec G\cdot\vec u}}=1-\frac16|\vec G|^2\overline{u^2}\approx e^{-\frac16|\vec G|^2\overline{u^2}}$ (first two terms of the exponential; real and $<1$). $I\propto|S|^2$: $I=I_0e^{-\frac13|\vec G|^2\overline{u^2}}$. The peaks stay sharp and at the same place; they lose intensity, more at large $|\vec G|$ and high $T$.'),
    (r'(b) Niobium at the melting point.', r'BCC nearest-neighbour distance $\frac{\sqrt3}{2}a$, so $\sqrt{\overline{u^2}}=0.2\cdot\frac{\sqrt3}2a=\frac{\sqrt3}{10}a$, $\overline{u^2}=\frac{3a^2}{100}$. $|\vec G|^2=\frac{8\pi^2}{a^2}$. $D=\exp\left[-\frac13\cdot\frac{8\pi^2}{a^2}\cdot\frac{3a^2}{100}\right]=\exp\left[-\frac{\pi^2}{12.5}\right]=e^{-0.79}=0.45$. Independent of $a$: valid for the (110) reflection of every BCC crystal.')],
   [r'expansion to second order, linear term averages to zero', r'$\overline{\cos^2\Theta}=\frac13$', r'$D=e^{-|\vec G|^2\overline{u^2}/3}$ in the intensity ($e^{-\ldots/6}$ in the amplitude)', r'Lindemann: $\sqrt{\overline{u^2}}=\frac{\sqrt3}{10}a$', r'$D=e^{-0.79}=0.45$'], source=EX(5, 4)),
])

S(6, 'Low-dimensional electrons, graphene, free electrons at the zone boundary', '2026-10-30', [
 Q(1, 'Low-dimensional electron systems', 'fermi-sea', ['ss-fer-L1', 'ss-fer-D1', 'ss-fer-c1', 'ss-fer-Q1'],
   r'Electrons confined to 2D (MOSFET interface), 1D (quantum wire) or 0D (quantum dot), $E(k)=\frac{\hbar^2k^2}{2m}$. (a) Density of states in 2D and 1D (with spin); what is it in 0D? Plot them against energy. (b) Fermi wave vector $k_F$ in 1D and 2D as a function of the electron density $n$ (per length, per area).',
   [(r'(a) 2D.', r'$N(E)=2\frac{A}{(2\pi)^2}\int_0^{k(E)}2\pi k\,dk$; with $dE=\frac{\hbar^2}{m}k\,dk$: $N(E)=\frac{Am}{\pi\hbar^2}E$ and $D_{2D}(E)=\frac1A\frac{dN}{dE}=\frac{m}{\pi\hbar^2}$: constant.'),
    (r'(a) 1D and 0D.', r'$N(E)=2\frac{L}{2\pi}\int_{-k(E)}^{k(E)}dk=\frac{2L}{\pi}\sqrt{\frac{2mE}{\hbar^2}}$, $D_{1D}(E)=\frac1L\frac{dN}{dE}=\frac1\pi\sqrt{\frac{2m}{\hbar^2}}\frac1{\sqrt E}$: diverges at the band bottom (van Hove). 0D: a single level, $D_{0D}(E)=2\delta(E-E_0)$.<br>' + F('s6q1-dos', 'Density of states against energy: 0D a spike, 1D falling as one over square root of E, 2D constant, 3D rising as square root of E', 380)),
    (r'(b) Fermi wave vectors.', r'2D: $n_{2D}=\frac{N(E_F)}{A}=\frac{m}{\pi\hbar^2}\frac{\hbar^2k_F^2}{2m}=\frac{k_F^2}{2\pi}$, $k_F=\sqrt{2\pi n_{2D}}$. 1D: $n_{1D}=\frac{2k_F}{\pi}$, $k_F=\frac\pi2n_{1D}$. (3D for comparison: $k_F=(3\pi^2n)^{1/3}$, script eq. 3.22.)')],
   [r'$D_{2D}=m/\pi\hbar^2$, constant', r'$D_{1D}\propto E^{-1/2}$', r'$D_{0D}=2\delta(E-E_0)$', r'$k_F=\sqrt{2\pi n_{2D}}$ and $k_F=\frac\pi2n_{1D}$'], source=EX(6, 1)),
 Q(2, 'Electron dispersion, density of states and heat capacity of graphene', 'fermi-sea', ['ss-fer-L1', 'ss-fer-L2', 'ss-fer-c2', 'ss-deg-L1'],
   r'Graphene near the Fermi level: $\epsilon(\mathbf k)=\pm c|\mathbf k|$, $\epsilon_F=0$. (a) Sketch the dispersion. Which other particles have a linear dispersion? (b) Density of states $g(\epsilon)$ (spin and a two-fold valley degeneracy: factor 4 in total); it should be linear in $|\epsilon|$. (c) At temperature $T$, electrons within $k_BT$ below $\epsilon_F$ are excited and gain about $k_BT$. Show $n_{ex}\approx\frac12g(-k_BT)k_BT$ and find $\Delta E(T)=E(T)-E_0$. (d) Heat capacity $C_e(T)$.',
   [(r'(a) Dispersion.', r'Two cones touching at $(\mathbf k,\epsilon)=(0,0)$ (a V and an inverted V in a cut along $k_x$). Photons ($\omega=ck$) and low-energy acoustic phonons ($\omega=v_sk$) are linear too; here $c$ plays the role of $\hbar v_F$.'),
    (r'(b) Density of states.', r'As for the 2D Debye phonons (sheet 5 Q1) with degeneracy 4: $N=4\left(\frac L{2\pi}\right)^2\int_0^{k_0}2\pi k\,dk=4\left(\frac L{2\pi}\right)^2\int_0^{\epsilon_0}2\pi\frac{\epsilon}{c^2}d\epsilon$, so $g(\epsilon)=\frac{2L^2}{\pi c^2}\epsilon$ for $\epsilon>0$ and by symmetry $g(\epsilon)=\frac{2L^2}{\pi c^2}|\epsilon|$. It vanishes at the Fermi level.'),
    (r'(c) Excited electrons.', r'$g$ is linear and zero at $\epsilon_F=0$, so the number of states between $-k_BT$ and $0$ is a triangle of base $k_BT$ and height $g(-k_BT)$: $n_{ex}=\frac12g(-k_BT)k_BT=\frac{L^2k_B^2T^2}{\pi c^2}$. Each gains $\sim k_BT$: $E(T)-E_0=n_{ex}k_BT=\frac{L^2k_B^3T^3}{\pi c^2}$.'),
    (r'(d) Heat capacity.', r'$C_e=\frac{\partial E}{\partial T}=\frac{3L^2k_B^3T^2}{\pi c^2}\propto T^2$. Compare a metal: $g(\epsilon_F)$ finite, $n_{ex}\sim g(\epsilon_F)k_BT$, $C\propto T$. The $T^2$ is the 2D Debye law with $v\to c/\hbar$: same linear dispersion, same counting.')],
   [r'two cones; photons and acoustic phonons as analogues', r'$g(\epsilon)=\frac{2L^2}{\pi c^2}|\epsilon|$', r'triangle argument for $n_{ex}$', r'$\Delta E=\frac{L^2k_B^3T^3}{\pi c^2}$', r'$C_e\propto T^2$, not $\propto T$'], source=EX(6, 2)),
 Q(3, 'Energy of free electrons', 'fermi-sea', ['ss-fer-L2', 'ss-fer-c3', 'ss-fer-b1'],
   r'(a) Simple square lattice in 2D: show that a free electron at the corner of the first Brillouin zone has twice the kinetic energy of one at the centre of a side. (b) Simple cubic lattice in 3D: show that the energy at the corner is three times that at the centre of a face.',
   [(r'(a) Square lattice.', r'The reciprocal lattice is square with constant $2\pi/a$; the zone is the square $|k_x|,|k_y|\le\pi/a$. Side centre: $k_0=\frac\pi a$. Corner: $k_1=\sqrt2\frac{\pi}{a}=\sqrt2k_0$. $E_1=\frac{\hbar^2k_1^2}{2m}=2\frac{\hbar^2k_0^2}{2m}=2E_0$.'),
    (r'(b) Simple cubic.', r'Face centre $k_0=\frac\pi a$, corner $k_1=\sqrt3\frac\pi a$: $E_1=3E_0$. Why it matters: a divalent metal can only stay metallic if the gap at the face centre is smaller than this spread, so that states near the corners of the first zone remain empty while the second zone starts to fill.')],
   [r'zone boundary at $\pi/a$, side (face) centre $k_0=\pi/a$', r'corner $k_1=\sqrt2k_0$ (2D), $\sqrt3k_0$ (3D)', r'$E\propto k^2$: factors 2 and 3'], source=EX(6, 3)),
])
